{"schema_version":1,"seed":20261107,"selection_sha256":"dba6bd0684df7e8640530c5fbc1a1d329325ab4fb8db3275db3f4aaf6ccda0a2","corpus_sha256":"e958b61284b06f841e05d75f718f70471460036df9a6794d6a77e102454ac6dd","piece_format":"native_vocabulary_piece","tokens_representation":"Native vocabulary pieces; individual decodes are not aligned source spans.","documents":[{"id":"academic-en-0018","collection":"dataset/academic/en.jsonl","text":"Vibration isolation with an off-center suspended load\nA rigid platform is hung from four springs and carries a movable weight. Vertical translation and tilt become coupled when the weight is shifted away from the center. In a calculated frequency sweep, a sensor placed at one corner shows a deep response minimum that is absent from the platform's average motion. The minimum results from cancellation between translation and rotation, rather than from complete isolation of the load. Moving the sensor changes its apparent location. We derive the small-motion equations and verify that symmetry restores decoupling for a centered load. Spring damping is represented phenomenologically, and large-angle motion is excluded. The analysis illustrates why the location of a vibration measurement matters when comparing isolators. A single favorable trace does not capture the motion of the whole platform.","token_ids":[1086,100611,26258,1454,1420,2490,12619,30334,5174,1010,1065,32471,10393,1395,20434,1562,3946,64177,1321,34472,1261,50969,5954,1046,64334,18165,1321,36192,6127,18015,2200,1278,5954,1395,27477,5109,1562,1278,7034,1046,1656,1261,10407,8361,58276,1044,1261,12450,9236,1513,1925,15552,6122,1261,8378,4005,11835,1455,1395,29436,1562,1278,10393,1681,6931,12968,1046,1531,11835,3657,1562,55953,2396,18165,1321,16189,1044,6153,2224,1562,7662,26258,1307,1278,5174,1046,74276,1278,12450,5266,2246,17871,7285,1046,2837,42463,1278,3709,3049,15984,20607,1321,21294,1455,39665,3067,3097,108183,10898,1394,1261,40274,5174,1046,16825,80846,1395,12685,21348,25681,10